WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:03.269
-Our first guests tonight
have worked for decades

00:00:03.269 --> 00:00:04.536
in the highest levels
of politics,

00:00:04.536 --> 00:00:05.771
policy, and philanthropy.

00:00:05.771 --> 00:00:07.773
One has served as Senator,
Secretary of State,

00:00:07.773 --> 00:00:09.509
and won the popular vote
for president.

00:00:09.509 --> 00:00:11.877
The other is vice chair
of the Clinton Founda --

00:00:11.877 --> 00:00:14.481
That's just math, everybody.
Of the Clinton Foundation

00:00:14.481 --> 00:00:16.582
and a "New York Times"
best-selling author.

00:00:16.582 --> 00:00:20.219
Their docuseries "Gutsy" is
streaming now on Apple TV Plus.

00:00:20.219 --> 00:00:21.487
Let's take a look.

00:00:21.487 --> 00:00:23.789
-You got this.
Come up one more. Right.

00:00:23.789 --> 00:00:24.991
-Okay.
-Alright.

00:00:24.991 --> 00:00:26.592
Now hold --
hold with both hands.

00:00:26.592 --> 00:00:28.261
Put your body
in front of my body.

00:00:28.261 --> 00:00:29.729
-Alright. I'm ready.
-You ready?

00:00:29.729 --> 00:00:31.564
Circle, circle. There you go.

00:00:31.564 --> 00:00:33.432
Let's move.
One step forward. Go.

00:00:33.432 --> 00:00:35.134
-Wow. It's so heavy.

00:00:35.134 --> 00:00:37.169
-You have heart.
-Well, yeah.

00:00:37.169 --> 00:00:38.838
-Mom's like,
"You don't know my baby."

00:00:38.838 --> 00:00:40.106
[ Laughter ]

00:00:40.106 --> 00:00:41.774
-Please welcome back
to the show

00:00:41.774 --> 00:00:44.377
Hillary and Chelsea Clinton,
everyone.

00:00:44.377 --> 00:00:45.878
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:45.878 --> 00:00:55.555 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:55.555 --> 00:01:05.265 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:05.265 --> 00:01:08.501
Welcome back. I'm so happy
to have you both here.

00:01:08.501 --> 00:01:10.169
-We are so --
-We are happy to be here.

00:01:10.169 --> 00:01:13.072
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Oh, my gosh. Yes. Hi.

00:01:13.072 --> 00:01:14.573
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:14.573 --> 00:01:17.476
-I'm not alone in my happiness.

00:01:17.476 --> 00:01:19.311
I just got to ask right away,
having seen the clip,

00:01:19.311 --> 00:01:20.713
which is -- Be honest.

00:01:20.713 --> 00:01:22.681
Which is harder -- front of hose
or back of hose? Because...

00:01:22.681 --> 00:01:25.084
-Front of hose.
-Okay, thank you.

00:01:25.084 --> 00:01:27.119
And who actually
got to put on all the gear?

00:01:27.119 --> 00:01:29.622
-Yeah. I feel like
that was maybe a giveaway

00:01:29.622 --> 00:01:31.824
that your job
was more like a fire person.

00:01:31.824 --> 00:01:33.893
-Yes, thank you.

00:01:33.893 --> 00:01:36.062
They didn't even ask my mom.
-Really?

00:01:36.062 --> 00:01:37.930
-She didn't even
have to turn them down.

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:39.331
They just didn't ask her.

00:01:39.331 --> 00:01:40.867
-Now, would you have turned
them down if they had said,

00:01:40.867 --> 00:01:42.868
"You want to fire fight,"
don't you?

00:01:42.868 --> 00:01:44.471
-No, I would not
have turned them down.

00:01:44.471 --> 00:01:45.971
-She would have said,
"My daughter."

00:01:45.971 --> 00:01:48.407
-That's right. We did that
all through the series,

00:01:48.407 --> 00:01:52.178
which was so incredibly fun
for us to do together.

00:01:52.178 --> 00:01:55.147
And there would be
all these activities

00:01:55.147 --> 00:01:57.984
because we didn't want to just
have the usual interview show.

00:01:57.984 --> 00:01:59.418
-And the guests
set the activities.

00:01:59.418 --> 00:02:01.320
-Yeah, we said to the guests,
"What should we do?"

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:03.990
So when we knew
we were going to go out

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:05.191
to the training academy

00:02:05.191 --> 00:02:07.193
for the Fire Department
of New York

00:02:07.193 --> 00:02:10.129
to interview these
incredible women firefighters,

00:02:10.129 --> 00:02:14.333
including one of the very first
who was really a pioneer,

00:02:14.333 --> 00:02:16.302
we got out there, we said,
"Oh, so what do you want to do?"

00:02:16.302 --> 00:02:20.873
You know, he said, "Well,
we want you to put on the gear.

00:02:20.873 --> 00:02:23.542
And we won't put
all the gear on you.

00:02:23.542 --> 00:02:27.179
We'll only put 50 pounds
of the gear on you."

00:02:27.179 --> 00:02:29.482
And I said, "You know,
my daughter would really like

00:02:29.482 --> 00:02:31.016
to do that."

00:02:31.016 --> 00:02:33.118
-True story, true story.

00:02:33.118 --> 00:02:34.587
-Well, I think, you know,

00:02:34.587 --> 00:02:37.557
anytime, you know, a parent
can travel with a child,

00:02:37.557 --> 00:02:38.958
it's -- you know, it's a gift.
-Yes.

00:02:38.958 --> 00:02:40.426
-Especially when they're
adult children.

00:02:40.426 --> 00:02:41.594
-Right.
-That makes a huge difference.

00:02:41.594 --> 00:02:42.795
-It makes
a very big difference.

00:02:42.795 --> 00:02:45.865
-But I'm wondering
how traveling now together

00:02:45.865 --> 00:02:47.833
is compared to your time
traveling together

00:02:47.833 --> 00:02:49.668
as members of the first family.

00:02:49.668 --> 00:02:51.170
Is it massively different?

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:53.372
-Well, I mean, it's just
regular most of the time.

00:02:53.372 --> 00:02:56.910
We just get on airplanes at,
like, LaGuardia or Newark or JFK

00:02:56.910 --> 00:02:58.845
and go to wherever
we're going to go.

00:02:58.845 --> 00:03:01.714
Now, to be fair, I still
do have Secret Service

00:03:01.714 --> 00:03:05.418
because I was once first lady.

00:03:05.418 --> 00:03:07.353
So that's a little different

00:03:07.353 --> 00:03:09.688
because we can
count on getting a car

00:03:09.688 --> 00:03:11.390
and not standing
at the rental-car counter

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:13.493
wondering whether we're going
to get a car, you know?

00:03:13.493 --> 00:03:16.062
-Is there ever a Secret Service
agent looking at his phone

00:03:16.062 --> 00:03:17.429
saying, "The Uber.
It's so weird.

00:03:17.429 --> 00:03:20.833
Because it said five,
and now it says eight."

00:03:20.833 --> 00:03:22.835
[ Laughter ]

00:03:22.835 --> 00:03:25.671
-We're going to really talk
to them when they get here, yes.

00:03:25.671 --> 00:03:27.073
It was so much fun.

00:03:27.073 --> 00:03:29.475
-It also was similar, Seth,

00:03:29.475 --> 00:03:32.244
in the way that we've always
had a lot of fun together.

00:03:32.244 --> 00:03:34.614
We've always wanted to have
new experiences together.

00:03:34.614 --> 00:03:37.817
My parents have thankfully
been always not only encouraging

00:03:37.817 --> 00:03:40.286
but expecting me
to be curious about people

00:03:40.286 --> 00:03:41.787
and new experiences
and the world.

00:03:41.787 --> 00:03:44.290
And so in some ways
this felt like a continuation

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:47.059
and also different because,
you know, I was old enough

00:03:47.059 --> 00:03:48.895
to have a drink with my mom
at the end of the day.

00:03:48.895 --> 00:03:51.064
Which I wasn't 30 years ago.

00:03:51.064 --> 00:03:52.564
-Right.
-That's very true.

00:03:52.564 --> 00:03:54.000
-And I would imagine, too,

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:55.834
because of the life
that you've lived,

00:03:55.834 --> 00:03:58.437
you have to make
pretty extreme choices

00:03:58.437 --> 00:04:00.673
to find yourself
in a new experiences,

00:04:00.673 --> 00:04:02.608
which you did in Paris.

00:04:02.608 --> 00:04:04.576
-We did. We did in Paris, yes.

00:04:04.576 --> 00:04:06.111
-So, Hillary,
I'm going to show a photo.

00:04:06.111 --> 00:04:07.913
-Oh, come on. Okay.
-In the moment this happened,

00:04:07.913 --> 00:04:09.983
you on screen even said...

00:04:09.983 --> 00:04:12.351
-What have I gotten myself into?

00:04:12.351 --> 00:04:15.421
-I think this still
catches that exact moment.

00:04:15.421 --> 00:04:17.290
[ Laughter ]

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:20.225
[ Applause ]

00:04:20.225 --> 00:04:22.361
-Well --
-The red nose

00:04:22.361 --> 00:04:24.730
that launched a thousand
right-wing memes.

00:04:24.730 --> 00:04:27.133
-Exactly. I'm already using it

00:04:27.133 --> 00:04:30.570
as a meme in response
to texts from my wife.

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:33.273
-Well, I'm always glad
to be of service.

00:04:33.273 --> 00:04:36.776
And, you know,
when it was suggested

00:04:36.776 --> 00:04:39.479
that we go to Paris
to clown school,

00:04:39.479 --> 00:04:41.614
because we were doing an --

00:04:41.614 --> 00:04:44.383
a whole episode
about women are funny,

00:04:44.383 --> 00:04:45.918
like, women get the last laugh.

00:04:45.918 --> 00:04:48.253
Because when I was growing up,
everybody said,

00:04:48.253 --> 00:04:50.222
"Oh, women aren't funny"
and I said, "Well, I watch

00:04:50.222 --> 00:04:52.558
'I Love Lucy' all the time.
I think she's really funny."

00:04:52.558 --> 00:04:54.160
-Yeah.
-And so, we said,

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:55.394
"Okay, we'll go."

00:04:55.394 --> 00:04:57.630
But I have to confess.
I was pretty skeptical.

00:04:57.630 --> 00:05:00.065
It was one of the most
amazing experiences.

00:05:00.065 --> 00:05:02.735
Talk about experiences
I did not have as first lady.

00:05:02.735 --> 00:05:03.870
-This is the clown school.

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:06.271
-Going to clown school.
Yeah, yeah.

00:05:06.271 --> 00:05:09.342
I felt like I was, like,
in a comic all the time,

00:05:09.342 --> 00:05:12.278
but I wasn't actually wearing a
red nose when I was first lady.

00:05:12.278 --> 00:05:14.346
-That must be like
when somebody comes over to you

00:05:14.346 --> 00:05:15.614
with a thing of seltzer.

00:05:15.614 --> 00:05:17.216
That must be tricky
for Secret Service.

00:05:17.216 --> 00:05:18.350
-Yeah, that --

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:20.653
-Is this clowning?!
Is this an attack?!

00:05:20.653 --> 00:05:22.621
-"Put the seltzer in the bag."

00:05:22.621 --> 00:05:25.791
But there we were literally
on the stage of the Moulin Rouge

00:05:25.791 --> 00:05:27.760
actually taking
a clowning lesson.

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:30.496
And people like Sacha Baron
Cohen and Emma Thompson --

00:05:30.496 --> 00:05:32.798
people you know have gone
to this clowning school.

00:05:32.798 --> 00:05:34.433
We think you should go.

00:05:34.433 --> 00:05:35.768
-Oh, really?
-Yeah.

00:05:35.768 --> 00:05:37.236
-Yeah.
-Thank you. Thank you.

00:05:37.236 --> 00:05:39.138
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:39.138 --> 00:05:41.607
-I know --
I know many who would tell you,

00:05:41.607 --> 00:05:43.910
"Oh, he's clown enough."

00:05:43.910 --> 00:05:45.711
-But until you put on
that red nose

00:05:45.711 --> 00:05:47.580
and have that sense
of vulnerability

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:50.049
but also possibility, who knows?

00:05:50.049 --> 00:05:54.119
-You actually filmed an episode
with my colleague Amber Ruffin.

00:05:54.119 --> 00:05:55.788
-Yes.
-Yes.

00:05:55.788 --> 00:05:58.590
-And you actually spent time
with her at her home, I believe.

00:05:58.590 --> 00:06:00.192
-We did.

00:06:00.192 --> 00:06:01.727
-How did you find your time
with Amber?

00:06:01.727 --> 00:06:02.828
I can certainly speak to mine,

00:06:02.828 --> 00:06:04.263
but how did you enjoy her,
Chelsea?

00:06:04.263 --> 00:06:07.299
-Hilarious, warm.

00:06:07.299 --> 00:06:10.502
She made us feel like
we could be funny.

00:06:10.502 --> 00:06:12.304
You know, we think we're funny,

00:06:12.304 --> 00:06:15.107
but then she made us think that
she would maybe find us funny.

00:06:15.107 --> 00:06:17.075
And she laughed a lot,
so hopefully

00:06:17.075 --> 00:06:19.045
she did and we succeeded.

00:06:19.045 --> 00:06:22.147
I just -- You know, Seth,
I found her not only

00:06:22.147 --> 00:06:27.820
so warm and generous
and supportive and encouraging,

00:06:27.820 --> 00:06:29.755
I also found her really wise.

00:06:29.755 --> 00:06:31.591
Like, she just had a real wisdom

00:06:31.591 --> 00:06:33.459
about how she thought
about comedy,

00:06:33.459 --> 00:06:36.929
how she thought about her show
and who was part of her show.

00:06:36.929 --> 00:06:39.765
We got to be part
of her writers' room.

00:06:39.765 --> 00:06:42.167
She wrote a really
hilarious skit for my mother

00:06:42.167 --> 00:06:45.270
in a pants-suit faceoff.

00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:48.206
And I just thought like,
"Oh, this is wise."

00:06:48.206 --> 00:06:49.775
She's wise and funny.

00:06:49.775 --> 00:06:52.511
-This is gonna make
her impossible to be around,

00:06:52.511 --> 00:06:54.179
everything you just said.
-Oh, sorry.

00:06:54.179 --> 00:06:56.115
-The amount she's gonna
stand up in meetings now

00:06:56.115 --> 00:07:01.053
and be like,
"If you'll remember, I'm wise!"

00:07:01.053 --> 00:07:03.188
I have a million more questions
for you guys.

00:07:03.188 --> 00:07:05.958
We're gonna take a quick break.
We'll be right back with more

00:07:05.958 --> 00:07:07.860
from Hillary
and Chelsea Clinton.

